Tests for the C++11 library (doc/stdcxx-cxx11.md, doc/stdcxx-cxx11-TODO.md)

  fetch-libcxx.sh DIR    downloads libc++ 8.0.1; its tests are the check
  lcxx.py PATH...        runs libc++ tests with pathCC -fpsclang
                         (--host: with the host clang++ instead)
  hostcc.sh ...          host clang++ with the suite's stdcxx and runtime

  export LIBCXX_SRC=DIR PATH64_STAGE=BUILD/Xcompiler
  ./lcxx.py -v utilities/tuple

Regular native Linux x86-64 regression target:
  ninja -C BUILD stdcxx-check

This runs every original *.pass.cpp in C++11/C++14 at -O0/-O2. It fails for
unexpected compile/run failures or timeouts, and saves JSON under
BUILD/libstdcxx/check-results. It builds the staged compiler/runtime first.
Optionally configure the approved libc++ 8.0.1 reference checkpoint:
  cmake -S . -B BUILD -DPATH64_STDCXX_REFERENCE=/path/to/libcxx-8.0.1
  ninja -C BUILD stdcxx-check

checkpoint.txt lists reference paths expected to pass. Reference markers still
exclude unavailable language modes/locales and expected Clang-4 failures. No
reference sources are copied into the repository. Set PATH64_STDCXX_CHECK_JOBS
in CMake to control parallel compiler processes (default 4).
check.py also accepts --host, --manifest and --output for direct runs. Host
Clang cannot run the compiler-specific invalid-new[] bound test unchanged;
--host is primarily for comparing selected reference/library tests via lcxx.py.
lcxx.py --strict gives CI exit status; exploratory runs retain the existing
zero status on test failures. Missing paths and empty suites always fail.
Original tests passed by absolute path do not require a libc++ checkout.
Compilation defaults to a 120-second limit (--compile-timeout); timeouts stop
compiler/test process groups. test_runner.py checks strict status, exclusions,
timeouts, duplicate selection and invalid selections with a fake compiler.

Original regression tests can be passed by absolute path:
  ./lcxx.py "$PWD/compiler-lifetime.pass.cpp" "$PWD/compiler-virtual-base.pass.cpp"
  ./lcxx.py "$PWD/vector-exceptions.pass.cpp" "$PWD/vector-bool.pass.cpp"
  ./lcxx.py "$PWD/deque-exceptions.pass.cpp" "$PWD/compiler-return-object.pass.cpp"
  ./lcxx.py "$PWD/compiler-unwind.pass.cpp"
  ./lcxx.py -std c++14 "$PWD/sized-delete.pass.cpp"
  ./lcxx.py "$PWD/phase3-algorithms.pass.cpp"
  ./lcxx.py "$PWD/phase3-move-iterators.pass.cpp" "$PWD/compiler-short-circuit.pass.cpp"
  ./lcxx.py "$PWD/move-sorting.pass.cpp" "$PWD/partition-exceptions.pass.cpp"
  ./lcxx.py "$PWD/compiler-member-return.pass.cpp"
  ./lcxx.py "$PWD/compiler-member-init-unwind.pass.cpp" "$PWD/compiler-constructor-cleanup.pass.cpp"
  ./lcxx.py language.support/support.dynamic/alloc.errors "$PWD/new-handler.pass.cpp"

Use --timeout 180 for the deque range-insertion stress test at -O0. The
runner's default per-test runtime limit remains 30 seconds.

The libc++ vector tests erase_iter, erase_iter_iter and vector.special/swap
assume class iterators. With stdcxx's raw pointers, temporary test copies need
qualified std::distance and std::next, and std::prev(v.end()) in place of
--v.end(). Keep the reference sources unchanged and report their compile
failures separately from these adapted checks.

phase3-algorithms.pass.cpp checks min/max tie selection, the minmax_element
comparison bound on exhaustive small sequences through forward iterators,
negative copy counts, numeric generation, bounded comparisons, and C++14
constant evaluation. It needs no <random> implementation.

phase3-move-iterators.pass.cpp checks all insertion adapters with move-only
deque elements and the rotated sequence/return iterator. The reference front
insertion test uses list, whose move insertion is a separate container TODO.
compiler-short-circuit.pass.cpp checks conditional reference materialization,
null pointers, nested operands, exceptions and class temporary destruction.

move-sorting.pass.cpp checks move-only sort, heap and partial-sort contracts,
identity preservation, comparison bounds and packed-bit proxy iterators.
partition-exceptions.pass.cpp substitutes the raw temporary-buffer provider to
check full, partial and failed allocations, stable order and resource cleanup
when moves or predicates throw. It exercises the public stable_partition API.
compiler-member-return.pass.cpp checks calls returning trivial classes into
members at different offsets, and side effects in scalar/trivial destruction.

compiler-member-init-unwind.pass.cpp is the formerly failing aggregate-member
initializer unwind repro. compiler-constructor-cleanup.pass.cpp adds destruction
order, references, base/delegating construction, inner catches and temporary
argument checks. new-handler.pass.cpp checks synchronized get/set operations,
callback reentrancy, throwing/nothrow allocation failure and the new error type.

compiler-array-length.pass.cpp checks invalid new[] bounds, payload/cookie
limits, initializer counts, one-time bound evaluation, nonthrowing null results
and ordinary allocation failure. This exercises psclang's own bound lowering;
other compiler frontends can select different exceptions over this runtime.

compiler-loop-cleanup.pass.cpp checks destruction on break and continue,
including nested loop/switch targets, fallthrough, enclosing objects, and
reverse construction order. It also runs in C++98 (excluding range-for).

nth-element.pass.cpp checks move-only selection at every position, ascending
and descending comparisons, duplicate-heavy linear scans, packed-bit proxies,
object identities and resource cleanup after comparison exceptions.

stable-sorting.pass.cpp checks move-only stable_sort with full, partial and
failed temporary-buffer allocation; ascending/descending stable identity
ordering; packed-bit proxies; comparison bounds; and resource cleanup when
moves or comparisons throw. Its buffer provider substitutes only raw storage.

c-library.pass.cpp checks the C++11 C-header interfaces together: native integer
type identity and macros, formatting/parsing, math overloads and ADL, floating
classification/rounding/exception flags, and stateful UTF-16/32 conversion.
The supplementary Unicode checks need C.UTF-8; ASCII checks always run.
compiler-limits-constants.pass.cpp is the promoted psclang regression for
__rw's numeric-limits constants, now referenced with their proper ABI names.

string-conversions.pass.cpp checks narrow/wide numeric conversion boundaries,
base prefixes, partial input, embedded NULs, invalid/range exceptions, consumed
positions and errno restoration. Formatting checks include signed zero and
maximum double/long-double output beyond the initial buffer. It also checks
the C++11 stdio and stdlib numeric exports used by this work.

unicode-strings.pass.cpp checks UTF-16/32 trait types, constexpr scalar
operations (including narrow/wide traits), bounded searches against a protected
memory page, overlapping moves, string aliases, copies/mutation, modifiers,
embedded NULs and noncharacters, surrogate/code-point storage, and hashes.
The protected-page checks use the Linux mmap/mprotect interfaces.

compiler-variable-linkage.pass.cpp checks namespace variables inside explicit
C++ linkage blocks, shared C linkage, static members, guarded locals and template
locals. Compile it with LINKAGE_PROVIDER_ONLY to produce the definitions object;
compile with LINKAGE_CONSUMER_ONLY and link that object to check a second
compiler against it. Default mode provides the usual single-file regression.

compiler-catch-cleanup.pass.cpp checks C++98/11/14 catch-by-value construction,
handler-scope destruction, scalar/void returns, loop exits, empty handlers,
throwing parameter destructors and default-argument lifetimes. A subprocess
checks that failure while copying a catch parameter terminates execution.

exception-handles.pass.cpp checks captured exception ownership, repeated
cross-thread propagation and identity, catch-copy independence and handler
exits, copy-constructor failures, and nested runtime_error propagation. Forked
subprocesses validate required termination without ending the runner process.
The full support.exception directory includes an unmarked C++17
uncaught_exceptions test; use propagation and except.nested for the C++11/14
exception-handle/nesting reference batch.

bitset-modern.pass.cpp checks constexpr construction, word boundaries, oversized
shifts, conversion overflow, hashing and bounded arrays against a protected page.
stream-manipulators.pass.cpp checks derived rvalue streams, narrow/wide time,
custom money facets, facet exceptions, failed output and hexadecimal precision.
compiler-narrow-return.pass.cpp checks poisoned upper register bits on x86-64
bool/char/short returns. NARROW_PROVIDER_ONLY and NARROW_CONSUMER_ONLY split
it into independently compiled objects for ABI interoperability checks.

compiler-wide-mask.pass.cpp checks 64-bit unsigned mask arithmetic around the
32-bit boundary, including conditional shift counts and canonical constants.

stream-moves.pass.cpp checks narrow/wide string-buffer ownership, get/put
positions, formatting/user storage/callback transfer, swaps and moved-from reuse.
stream-base-moves.pass.cpp exercises protected base moves/swaps and stream-buffer
copy/swap without optional locales; a virtual basic_ios must be moved once.
file-stream-moves.pass.cpp checks buffered input and output, replacing an open
destination, moved-from reuse and caller-owned buffers using unique temp files.
stream-buffer-position.pass.cpp is also runnable in C++98 and verifies that
input mode does not imply output-at-end; ate still starts output at the end.
ifstream reference move/swap tests require test.dat and test2.dat in the working
directory. Other file-buffer tests include platform_support.h and now use the original
codecvt header. The public flag and string-buffer allocator restrictions are
cleared by the 71-test compatibility checkpoint below.

stream-allocators.pass.cpp uses non-default-constructible tagged allocators to
check ownership, propagation, unequal moves, narrow/wide buffers, caller-owned
buffers and rollback after allocation failure. It asserts that all allocations
are released by a matching allocator with the original element count.
stream-flags.pass.cpp runs in C++98 too: public flag values, setter return values,
radix formatting/parsing, automatic detection, explicit locking extensions and
stdio synchronization must remain consistent with the internal flag storage.
The base/string/fmtflags reference batch now passes 71/71 (eight locale skips).
All sixteen numeric-facet references now pass as well.

numeric-facets.pass.cpp checks generic input iterators with deleted comma,
unsigned overflow/negative input, failed-conversion values, bool state, float
forms, long tokens, grouping failures and COW string front/back mutation.
compiler-parenthesized-member.pass.cpp is C++98-compatible and checks direct,
indirect-object and const calls through parenthesized member expressions.
codecvt-unicode.pass.cpp checks Unicode scalar boundaries, malformed UTF-8,
partial buffers, UTF-16 units, BOM modes, byte order, filename overloads and
wide-file integration. The approved locale.stdcvt references pass 24/24.

system-error.pass.cpp checks ADL enum customization, custom category equivalence,
messages, errno preservation, hashes, threaded singleton use, copying and throws.
ERROR_PROVIDER_ONLY/ERROR_CONSUMER_ONLY split the file for cross-compiler links:
category pointers, returned codes and thrown errors must agree across objects.
compiler-virtual-redeclaration.pass.cpp checks canonical virtual methods, calls
through returned references, one object evaluation, member pointers and deletion.
It also runs in C++98. The reference system-error/category checkpoint is 60/60
with one C++17 variable-trait exclusion. The two modern ios_base::failure references now pass as well.

compiler-constant-vptr.pass.cpp checks constant polymorphic globals and locals,
multiple bases, nested fields, bitfields, early dispatch and array destruction.
VPTR_PROVIDER_ONLY/VPTR_CONSUMER_ONLY split it for mixed compiler links.
ios-failure.pass.cpp checks inheritance, stored codes, allocation-free copies,
actual stream throws and exception_ptr ownership after thread exit. Compile
its ios-failure-provider.cpp in C++98 and the test with FAILURE_CONSUMER_ONLY
in C++11/14 to exercise legacy-provider/modern-consumer links. Rebuild all
clients with the changed exception ABI before running these tests.
compiler-unwind-registers.pass.cpp checks x86-64 callee-saved registers across
exception unwinding, including optimized code. UNWIND_PROVIDER_ONLY and
UNWIND_CONSUMER_ONLY support mixed compiler links; it also runs in C++98.

list-insertion.pass.cpp checks move-only and immobile emplacement, output
insertion adapters, const-iterator insertion, allocator construction hooks,
node reuse after exceptions and buffer ownership during moves. Moves transfer
storage without allocation. The embedded sentinel remains valid after moves;
allocation failure during source reuse leaves the source empty and reusable.
compiler-empty-assignment.pass.cpp checks empty-base copy/move assignment,
including operand effects, assignment-result references and explicit operator
calls, without overwriting overlapping derived storage. It also runs in C++98.

list-modern.pass.cpp checks allocator selection/propagation, copy/move and
allocator-extended constructors, initializer lists, const-iterator modifiers,
aliased assignment, minimal allocators, exact allocation ownership, rollback,
noexcept moves and operations on the empty moved-from state. It runs with node
buffering enabled and disabled. The unmodified libc++ list suite passes 54/60
in the default pooled mode and 60/60 with _RWSTD_NO_LIST_NODE_BUFFER (C++14 -O2,
both compilers; three reference exclusions). The six pooled-mode failures are
allocation-granularity/budget assumptions, detailed in the TODO; they are not
silently excluded by lcxx.py. Buffered splice/merge now preserve node identity;
shared pool references retain nodes after their source container is destroyed.
The C++11 list/node ABI changed; rebuild clients and keep C++98 lists separate.
container-length-errors.pass.cpp checks oversized vector, vector<bool>, deque
and list operations throw length_error with a valid message and retain the
original contents. This exercises the diagnostic formatting paths too.

list-transfer.pass.cpp checks allocation-free default construction/splice,
const-iterator and rvalue overloads, saved iterators/addresses, immobile stable
merge/sort/reverse, merge comparison bounds and comparator exceptions. It checks
both source/destination destruction orders, reuses nodes from dead source pools,
and destroys four partitions sharing pool storage concurrently. Compile
list-transfer-provider.cpp with one compiler in C++11, and the regression with
TRANSFER_CONSUMER_ONLY using the other compiler in C++14, for mixed ownership
checks. Both directions pass at -O0/-O2 with the new headers.
compiler-union-destruction.pass.cpp checks named and anonymous union lifetimes:
only explicit user code destroys active variants, while enclosing objects still
destroy their ordinary members. It covers the destructor link failure exposed
by unbuffered list nodes.
ios-failure.pass.cpp also checks four concurrent copy/assignment loops while
allocation is disabled; heap-shared message counts must remain atomic in TLS
builds.

static-guard.pass.cpp checks the ABI completion byte, explicit abort/retry,
32 rounds of eight contending threads, and publication of a compiler-generated
local static. Runtime guard completion and lock state must occupy distinct
words; returning after a contended completed initialization must unlock.
static-initializer-retry.pass.cpp checks compiler-generated abort/retry, cleanup
ordering, nested statics and catches, throwing argument constructors/destructors,
direct throw expressions, C++14 static lambda captures and eight contenders.
The failed attempt must finish its cleanup before another thread can retry;
only a successful initialization registers the static object's destructor.
For mixed compiler checks, compile static-retry-provider.cpp in C++11 with one
compiler and this regression in C++14 with STATIC_RETRY_CONSUMER_ONLY using the
other compiler, then link the objects. Both directions pass at -O0/-O2.
